// EXPECT: `HostListen` implements `Listen`, which is not allowed on the WEB platform
//
// The same refusal on the one platform whose grant set is otherwise the odd
// one out. A page grants three effects nothing else does, so "WEB has it and
// the rest do not" is the shape a reader expects here — and a page is also the
// platform that is *still* refused `Listen` now that `core/net/server` has an
// acceptor behind it, because a document does not hold a port open. This
// fixture was written before the grant landed and asserted the same thing then;
// that it did not move is the whole of what it is for.
//
// **`Sockets` and `WebSocketClient` are bound here and are not refused**, and
// that is the other half of the case rather than an oversight. They used to be
// refused beside `Listen`, on the ground that a page neither accepts
// connections nor holds one to push on. The second clause stopped being true
// when a page could dial: `core/net/websocket` mints a socket on every
// platform, so writing on one belongs on every platform too. What a page still
// cannot do is *accept*, and that is the one line left with a caret under it.
//
// PLATFORM: WEB — and the forged struct is refused with the value, as
// everywhere else a grant is withheld: `Listen: host.HostListen {}` is not the
// way round it either.
from "core/effect" import { Allocator, Listen, Sockets, Stdout, WebSocketClient };
from "core/host" import * as host;
from "core/io" import * as io;
from "ui/effect" import { Ui, Watch };

export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
  let ctx = context {
    Allocator: host.alloc,
    Stdout: host.stdout,
    Listen: host.HostListen {},
    Sockets: host.sockets,
    WebSocketClient: host.websocketClient,
    Ui: host.ui,
    Watch: host.watch,
  };
  let _ = io.println(ctx, "no page accepts one either").ignore();
  .Ok(())
}
